A||||10||DougKraft||Tires for 14" stock rims||||1||
Z||000000||DougKraft||12-17-2001||03:12 PM||||I have an 86 fiero SE with a V6 with the stock 14" rims and I would like to use a larger tire (outside of the standard 215-60-14) using the original factory rims <BR>that came on the car. The options I am considering are:<P>Front 195-70-14 or 205-70-14<BR>Rear 235-60-14<P>or<P>225-60-14 all the way around (doesn't seem to be a popular tire size)<P>1. Are either of these options a "fit" considering handling or rubbing on any part of the suspension?<BR>2. Secondly, are there any tires that anyone would recommend?<BR>3. Last should I just stay with the 215-60-14 standard tires?<P>Thankyou!<P>Doug Kraft<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||tesmith66||12-17-2001||03:23 PM||||I would stick with the factory tire size. The 70 series tires will be taller which will cause four problems:<P>They will cause your VSS to send the wrong signal to your speedo and computer<BR>There will be too much sidewall flex and the handling will suffer<BR>There are no speed rated tires in that series<BR>All 70 series tires are made for your grandma's Buick and are ugly. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>If you are trying to fill in that huge gap between the tire and wheel opening, I would suggest lowering the car. That will look better as well as improve handling. If you don't want to lower the car, then go with a 15" wheel with a 50 series tire or a 16" wheel with a 40 series tire. You don't want to change the overall tire diameter.<P>I have the 14" factory wheels with 215/60 14 B.F. Goodrich Radial T/As on my 86 SE. The car is lowered about 2".<P><BR>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe<p>[This message has been edited by tesmith66 (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnF||12-17-2001||06:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tesmith66:<BR><B>I would stick with the factory tire size. The 70 series tires will be taller which will cause four problems:<P>They will cause your VSS to send the wrong signal to your speedo and computer<BR>There will be too much sidewall flex and the handling will suffer<BR>There are no speed rated tires in that series<BR>All 70 series tires are made for your grandma's Buick and are ugly.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>FYI - the GM factory stickerd tire for '88 standard 2.5L notchback is 185/75-14.  I have had this size tire on this car since Nov. '87.<P>Does the VSS know the difference, probably not that anyone would notice.<P>Speed rated?  Who cares? At our highway speeds, tires will only last about 60,000 miles regardless of rating.<P>No doubt handling suffers but ride certainly improves.  <P>Are they ugly, I don't think so.  They fill the standard wheel well nicely.  The '85GT I have sitting next to it has the GM recommended 215/60-14 installed and the wheel well looks empty. It does handle better, though!<P>Bottom line is just as GM suggested, it is personal preference.  For the GT/SE, they suggested a "performace" size.  For the standard coupe, they suggested a "ride improving" size.<P>||||reg||10
Z||000003||My7Fieros||12-17-2001||06:53 PM||||I was curious to. My 86 Se v6 has larger tires than my Indy. Of course my SE is at the Fiero Factory right now, so I dont have the car here to look at the tires....its dark outside and Im too lazy to go look at the tires on my Indy to lol. But I did notice the tires on the Indy are noticably smaller than the SE...looks silly IMHO. Im wanting to put the same size tire on it thats on my SE.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||12-17-2001||08:56 PM||||There were 2 different size tires available on the 14" wheels.<BR>195/70R14 ~ This size was used on the SC's and SE's as the "Base" tire.<BR>215/60R14 ~ This was the "optional" tire size. It seems that this size tire was added with the WS6 suspension option. Although if someone wanted you could order them  seperately.<P>I don't know exactly what year used what size, But the Indy, 84/85 SE's, and 85GT's had the 60's. The 85 SE's without the V-6 had the 70's and so did the 86/87 SE's. The option in the SE's was the 60 series. Not sure what came on 88's at all. <P>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierobruiser||12-17-2001||10:23 PM||||Just looking at my brochures. 1984 standard size on the Coupe and Sport Coupe 185/80x13 the SE 195/70x14. Up to 215/60x14 were available on all but the base coupe. For 85 it was the same with 215/60x14 standard on the GT. In 1986 the standard tire on the C and SC was 185/75x14 with the SE still at 195/70x14. and the GT 215/60x14. I don't have an 87 brochure yet, but the 1988 catalogue lists the Coupe at 185/75x14 and the Formula and GT as 205/60x15 front and 215/60x15 rear.My 85 GT has stock tires but my daily driver came with 205/60/14 frt and 195/70x14 rears. I don't notice a lot of sidewall flex in normal hard street driving and I like the slight rake, but I will replace them sometime in the coming year with 205/50 or 60x15 on a set of mesh Beretta rims. <P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||12-18-2001||12:51 AM||||The 86 GT is wrong. The only GT's available in 86 were the Fastback version offered 1/2 way thru the model year and they all had the 15" wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Blacktree||12-18-2001||02:53 AM||||I prefer to use the 215/60-14 size with an "S" or "T" speed rating. It's definately an improvement in handling, and doesn't make the ride much more harsh. The 215/60-TR14 gets my vote.<P>I also tried a pair of 245/60-SR14 tires on the rear for a short while. Those things fill up the wheel wells nicely and get lotsa grip, but make the rear end really sloppy. The tires are so wide that they balloon out from the rims, allowing the rim to float around inside the tire. They look (and act) kinda like those balloon tires they use on drag racing cars.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A><p>[This message has been edited by Blacktree (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fierobruiser||12-19-2001||01:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>The 86 GT is wrong. The only GT's available in 86 were the Fastback version offered 1/2 way thru the model year and they all had the 15" wheels.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You are correct sir, I typoed that one.<P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000009||My7Fieros||12-19-2001||09:16 PM||||So Im assuming its ok to put the larger size tires on the Indy and get rid of those silly tiny tires?||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||12-20-2001||01:37 PM||||In my cave, there are now links to a couple tire calculators that will help you compare sizes and tell you how far off the speedo will be. The Miata.Net one will handle rim diameters from 12-23 inches.<P>What the calculators don't include is important....<P>changing the outside diameter of the tire will also change the Effective Ratio of the transmission. As I remember, a taller tire reduces effective ratio.<P>To keep this simple....<P>Lets say with a stock tire and a 3.33:1 final gear in the transmission, you've got an effective ratio of 3.33:1. (You actually don't but well say that for now.)<P>A taller tire would push that toward 3:1 maybe even into the 2.x:1 range depending on how much bigger the tire is.<P>A smaller tire would go the other way towards 4:1.<P>I could have them backwards but you get the idea. This will alter the amount of power actually launching the car.<P>I usually try to keep the tires within 1%-3% error of the originals. If you much go more than that you should consider installing one of those little circuits that lets you recalibrate the speedo to the new tires.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
